WEBVTT

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:07.290
<v Ira Glass>Harper High School, South Side of Chicago, first day of school this year, first thing in the morning, everybody gathers in the gym for a beginning of the year assembly.
<v Ira Glass>芝加哥南区的哈珀高中，今年开学的第一天，清晨时分，所有人聚集在体育馆参加开学典礼。

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:08.040
<v Ira Glass>[GYM CHATTER]
<v Ira Glass>[体育馆内的嘈杂声]

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:11.440
<v Ira Glass>The School's principal, Leonetta Sanders, is at the mic.
<v Ira Glass>学校校长莱奥内塔·桑德斯（Leonetta Sanders）站在麦克风前。

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.860
<v Leonetta Sanders>I need us to begin to quiet down.
<v Leonetta Sanders>我需要大家开始安静下来。

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:21.220
<v Ira Glass>Sitting all together in a group are the freshmen, looking the way that freshmen do on the first day of school, like they barely know who they are--
<v Ira Glass>一群一年级新生聚在一起坐着，看起来就像开学第一天的新生一样，仿佛连自己是谁都还不太清楚——

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:28.660
<v Leonetta Sanders>The Class of 2016, where you at? The Class of 2016, where you at?
<v Leonetta Sanders>2016届的学生，你们在哪儿？2016届的学生，你们在哪儿？

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:29.160
<v Leonetta Sanders>[CHEERING]
<v Leonetta Sanders>[欢呼声]

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:34.180
<v Ira Glass>Down in front, sitting together, are the seniors, looking the way seniors do on the first day of school--
<v Ira Glass>坐在前排的是高年级学生，看起来就像开学第一天的高年级学生一样——

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:40.410
<v Leonetta Sanders>And then my babies are here, my Class of 2013, (YELLING) where you at?
<v Leonetta Sanders>还有我的宝贝们，我的2013届学生，（大喊）你们在哪儿？

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:41.840
<v Leonetta Sanders>[CHEERING]
<v Leonetta Sanders>[欢呼声]

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:51.640
<v Ira Glass>The program is 1/3 pep rally, 2/3 business, introductions, and rules. Exactly the kind of first day stuff you'd expect at any school, till it's not.
<v Ira Glass>这场典礼三分之一是动员会，三分之二则是正事、介绍和校规。这正是你在任何学校都会预料到的开学第一天的情景，直到它变得不再寻常。

00:00:51.640 --> 00:01:06.340
<v Leonetta Sanders>Last year was a difficult year for most of us, for all of us in the Harper community. You know, and the freshman may not know, but we lost three students last year.
<v Leonetta Sanders>去年对我们大多数人，对哈珀社区的所有人来说，都是艰难的一年。你们知道，可能一年级新生不知道，但我们去年失去了三名学生。

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:24.100
<v Ira Glass>This is actually underplaying the bad news. Last year, 21 kids, current and recent Harper students, were wounded by gunshots. Five recent students died. And that is all on top of the three current students that Miss Sanders mentioned. Total, 29 shot, eight of them dead.
<v Ira Glass>这其实还是淡化了坏消息。去年，有21名哈珀在读及刚毕业的学生遭到枪击受伤。五名刚毕业的学生不幸身亡。这还不包括桑德斯校长提到的那三名在校生。总计29人中枪，其中8人死亡。

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:55.370
<v Leonetta Sanders>But we know that their spirits are with us. So right now, I just want to ask for a moment of silence and prayer as we think about and remember the students that have fallen. So at this time, I'm just going to ask that we take about 20, 30 seconds, just for a moment of silence for Marcus Nunn, Cedric Bell, and Shakaki Asphy, right now.
<v Leonetta Sanders>但我们知道他们的精神与我们同在。所以现在，我请求大家保持片刻沉默并进行祈祷，让我们怀念并追忆那些逝去的学生。此时此刻，我请求大家默哀20到30秒，为了马库斯·纳恩（Marcus Nunn）、塞德里克·贝尔（Cedric Bell）和沙卡基·阿斯菲（Shakaki Asphy），就在现在。

00:01:55.370 --> 00:02:11.150
<v Ira Glass>Watching this, it's hard not to think that if you grafted these facts onto another high school in a wealthier place, maybe a suburb, dozens of students shot, three of them killed-- in other places, that would be national news, right? We would all know the name of that school.
<v Ira Glass>看着这一幕，很难不想到，如果把这些事实放在富裕地区的另一所高中，比如郊区，几十名学生被枪击，其中三人死亡——在其他地方，这会成为全国新闻，对吧？我们都会知道那所学校的名字。

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:28.920
<v Ira Glass>It's worth noting that this is a gym filled with hundreds of teenagers who, this very same morning, have been asked over and over to be quiet. And who, like most teenagers, haven't exactly jumped to. When this moment comes, nothing moves.
<v Ira Glass>值得注意的是，这是一个挤满了数百名青少年的体育馆，就在这个早晨，他们被反复要求保持安静。像大多数青少年一样，他们并没有立刻照做。当这一刻来临时，所有人都静止不动了。

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.990
<v Leonetta Sanders>Praise God.
<v Leonetta Sanders>感谢上帝。

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.680
<v Ira Glass>And then, high school resumes.
<v Ira Glass>随后，高中生活继续。

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:39.740
<v Leonetta Sanders>At this time, again, for the freshmen and some new students that are here, my name is Leonetta Sanders. I am--
<v Leonetta Sanders>现在，再次向一年级新生和在座的一些新同学介绍，我叫莱奥内塔·桑德斯。我是——

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:58.850
<v Ira Glass>On Friday of this week, President Obama went to a Chicago high school and spoke about all the shootings happening in our cities. We've all heard so much lately about kids getting shot. Last weekend, there was a funeral, 15-year-old Hadiya Pendleton, an honor student who was shot in a park in mid-afternoon in Chicago, just a week after performing at an inaugural event in Washington.
<v Ira Glass>本周五，奥巴马总统去了芝加哥的一所高中，谈到了我们城市里发生的所有枪击事件。最近我们听到了太多关于孩子被枪杀的消息。上周末，举行了15岁的哈迪亚·彭德尔顿（Hadiya Pendleton）的葬礼，她是一名优等生，在芝加哥一个公园的午后被枪杀，而就在一周前，她还在华盛顿的就职典礼上表演过。

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:15.820
<v Ira Glass>Last year the number of murders in Chicago rose while in many other big cities like New York and Los Angeles, it held steady or fell. It was 506 dead in Chicago last year. But of course, these deaths aren't evenly distributed all over the city. The majority are in a handful of neighborhoods, like this one.
<v Ira Glass>去年芝加哥的谋杀案数量上升，而纽约和洛杉矶等许多其他大城市则保持平稳或下降。去年芝加哥有506人死亡。但当然，这些死亡并非均匀分布在整个城市。大多数发生在少数几个社区，就像这里。

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:53.140
<v Ira Glass>This is Englewood. Police statistics show that it is one of the most dangerous areas of the city. Though, if you're picturing some kind of chaotic, depressing, ghetto high school in the middle of all of that, Harper is anything but. Amidst boarded up houses and vacant lots, it is a four story, yellow brick building. The grounds are neat and beautiful. The halls, walls, classrooms, cafeteria-- everything is well taken care of. There's order. Between passing periods, the halls are kept clear. It's clean. You can tell the staff likes the kids. Even the security guards-- there are 16 for a student body of just 550 or so. They joke around.
<v Ira Glass>这里是恩格尔伍德（Englewood）。警察统计数据显示，这是该市最危险的地区之一。不过，如果你脑海中勾勒出的是那种身处乱世、令人沮丧、位于贫民窟中心的高中，哈珀完全不是那样。在被木板钉住的房屋和空地之间，这是一栋四层的黄色砖砌建筑。校园整洁美丽。走廊、墙壁、教室、食堂——一切都维护得很好。这里很有秩序。课间休息时，走廊保持畅通。这里很干净。你可以看出教职工很喜欢这些孩子。甚至连保安——550名左右的学生配有16名保安。他们会开玩笑。

00:03:53.140 --> 00:04:02.960
<v Man>Let's go, freshmen! Let's go! I have fresh meat. (LAUGHS) You can't be sleepy already. It just started.
<v Man>快走，新生们！快走！我有新鲜的肉。（笑声）你们可不能现在就犯困。这才刚开始呢。

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:13.840
<v Ira Glass>Principal Sanders sets the tone for the building. And just listen here to how she informs a girl who showed up on the first day of school out of uniform that the girl can never do that again.
<v Ira Glass>桑德斯校长为这栋建筑定下了基调。请听听她是如何告诉一个开学第一天没穿校服的女孩，这种事绝不能再发生。

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:22.340
<v Leonetta Sanders>Oh, my God! You look so cute! Too bad you can't wear that white shirt. But you all look so cute!
<v Leonetta Sanders>天哪！你看起来真可爱！可惜你不能穿那件白衬衫。但你们看起来都好可爱！

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:41.790
<v Ira Glass>That's classic Harper. You're reprimanded, but with love. One consequence of all the violence last year, Ms. Sanders and other administrators had to spend a certain amount of this first day back to school trying to convince worried parents that it is safe to send their kids back to school here this year. None of last year's shootings actually happened at the school.
<v Ira Glass>这就是经典的哈珀风格。你受到了训斥，但充满了爱。去年所有暴力事件的一个后果是，桑德斯女士和其他行政人员不得不花费开学第一天的一部分时间，试图说服担心的家长，把孩子送回这里上学是安全的。去年的枪击事件实际上没有一起发生在学校内。

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.260
<v Chad Adams>Can I talk to him on the phone?
<v Chad Adams>我能在电话里和他谈谈吗？

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.090
<v Mother>Nuh uh. He don't want to come back.
<v Mother>不行。他不想回来。

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:48.960
<v Ira Glass>This is assistant principal Chad Adams and a mom who wants to transfer her son out.
<v Ira Glass>这是副校长查德·亚当斯（Chad Adams）和一位想让儿子转学的母亲。

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.570
<v Chad Adams>He doesn't want to come back? But he's been with us for two years.
<v Chad Adams>他不想回来？但他已经在我们这里待了两年了。

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:52.280
<v Mother>I know.
<v Mother>我知道。

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:56.190
<v Chad Adams>And we've put so much time and work and love into him. We want him to stay.
<v Chad Adams>我们在他身上投入了那么多的时间、工作和爱。我们希望他留下来。

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:57.230
<v Mother>I know.
<v Mother>我知道。

00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:01.020
<v Chad Adams>And what was the reason? You told me that grandmother said something. What was she saying again.
<v Chad Adams>原因是什么？你告诉我他奶奶说了些什么。她是怎么说的？

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:07.370
<v Mother>She said she don't want him up here because the fighting and the stuff going on.
<v Mother>她说她不想让他来这儿，因为外面有打架和那些乱七八糟的事。

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:09.130
<v Chad Adams>Is she worried about the killings?
<v Chad Adams>她是担心杀戮吗？

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:10.740
<v Mother>Yep. That what she worried about.
<v Mother>是的。那就是她担心的。

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:12.540
<v Chad Adams>And the people shooting at everyone?
<v Chad Adams>还有那些向所有人开枪的人？

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:13.960
<v Mother>Yeah.
<v Mother>对。

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:22.650
<v Chad Adams>Yeah. So even if we've got someone to come to his house every morning and pick him up, like, if I drove over to his house every morning and picked him up, you still wouldn't want him to come?
<v Chad Adams>是啊。所以即使我们每天早上派人到他家接他，比如，如果我每天早上开车去他家接他，你还是不想让他来吗？

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:44.040
<v Ira Glass>This is not, by the way, a theoretical question. Administrators do pick up some kids and drive them to school. Here at our radio show, we first heard about Harper this summer, when our colleague, Linda Lutton, who covers education at our home station, WBEZ in Chicago, did a story about the death of a 16-year-old sophomore named Shakaki Asphy, who played on the school's basketball team.
<v Ira Glass>顺便说一句，这并非一个理论问题。行政人员确实会接一些孩子并开车送他们去学校。在我们的广播节目中，我们是在今年夏天第一次听说哈珀的。当时，我们报道教育的同事琳达·勒顿（Linda Lutton）在我们位于芝加哥的母台WBEZ做了一个故事，关于一名叫沙卡基·阿斯菲（Shakaki Asphy）的16岁高二女生的死亡，她是学校篮球队的成员。

00:05:44.040 --> 00:06:08.790
<v Ira Glass>Shakaki was shot while standing on a porch talking to a friend near the end of the school year. Her murder was such a blow to the staff and the students that even Principal Sanders-- who's been at the school for years and is definitely not easy to rattle-- even Principal Sanders said that it made her wonder if she could continue doing this work. She showed Linda this list that she'd been keeping, for a year at that point, of all the kids who had been shot from Harper. Shakaki was number 27.
<v Ira Glass>沙卡基在学期末站在门廊和朋友说话时被枪杀。她的谋杀案对教职工和学生来说是一个沉重的打击，以至于连桑德斯校长——她在这所学校工作多年，绝不是那种容易被动摇的人——连她都说这让她怀疑自己是否还能继续做这份工作。她给琳达看了那份她保存了一年的名单，上面记录了所有从哈珀遭到枪击的孩子。沙卡基是第27个。

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:29.810
<v Ira Glass>All of us here knew, of course, about the murders in Chicago. But when we heard Linda's story about this one school with 27 shootings, at that point, in a year, we thought this is a school that knows this problem in a way that most of us around the country never see, don't know. And we wondered, what if we spent a long period of time there, to witness what they're witnessing?
<v Ira Glass>我们当然都知道芝加哥的谋杀案。但当我们听到琳达关于这所学校一年内发生27起枪击事件的故事时，我们想，这是一所对这个问题有着我们全国大多数人从未见过、不了解的认知的学校。我们想，如果我们在那里待上一段时间，去亲眼见证他们所见证的一切，会怎样？

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:43.840
<v Ira Glass>Harper agreed to let us send three reporters in, starting at the beginning of this school year. And they gave us unusual access for more than five months, for a full semester. When violence struck, they let us record the administrators as the administrators jumped into action.
<v Ira Glass>哈珀同意让我们派三名记者从本学年开始入驻。他们在超过五个月的时间里——整整一个学期——给了我们非同寻常的采访权限。当暴力事件发生时，他们让我们记录了行政人员在行动时的表现。

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:58.560
<v Ira Glass>They let us into private, difficult meetings with parents and students. And we watched the staff try to recover from the terrible year that they had last year and try to make this year different. As you'll hear, they devote incredible energy to trying to keep their students alive.
<v Ira Glass>他们让我们参加了私下且艰难的家长和学生会议。我们看着教职工试图从去年可怕的一年中恢复过来，并努力让今年变得不同。正如你将听到的，他们投入了难以置信的精力，试图保护他们的学生活着。

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:06.390
<v Ira Glass>So much happened that we could not fit this all into one program. So what we're going to do is we're going to start our story at Harper this week. And we're going to continue it next week.
<v Ira Glass>发生的事情太多了，我们无法将这一切塞进一个节目中。所以我们将从本周开始讲述我们在哈珀的故事。并将在下周继续。

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:25.680
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. I hope you'll stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ的《美国生活》（This American Life），由公共广播国际（PRI）发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。希望你留下来继续收听。

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:50.490
<v Ira Glass>So our reporters today and next week are Linda Lutton, who covers Chicago schools for our home station, WBEZ, like I said; Ben Calhoun, from our staff, who used to be a Chicago reporter; and Alex Kotlowitz, who's written many times over the years about violence in Chicago and its effect on kids growing up there. And let's just start. Let's start by jumping ahead a little ways into the school year for Act One, Rules to Live By, from Linda.
<v Ira Glass>所以我们今天和下周的记者是琳达·勒顿（如我所说，她是WBEZ芝加哥学校报道的记者）、我们的员工本·卡尔霍恩（Ben Calhoun，他曾是芝加哥的记者），以及亚历克斯·科特洛维茨（Alex Kotlowitz，多年来多次撰写关于芝加哥暴力事件及其对成长中的孩子影响的文章）。让我们开始吧。让我们先跳到学年中段，开始第一幕《生存法则》（Rules to Live By），作者是琳达。

00:07:50.490 --> 00:08:04.610
<v Linda Lutton>Two weeks after that first day assembly, Assistant Principal Chad Adams, the same guy you heard trying to convince a mom to re-enroll her kid in Harper this year, is in the hallways. He spots a sophomore, a new kid, a transfer student named Jordan.
<v Linda Lutton>开学典礼两周后，副校长查德·亚当斯（就是你刚才听到试图说服一位妈妈让孩子重返哈珀的那个人）正在走廊里。他发现了一名高二学生，一个转校来的新生，名叫乔丹（Jordan）。

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:09.310
<v Chad Adams>So you were at Millburn? Did you know--
<v Chad Adams>所以你在米尔本（Millburn）上学？你知道——

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:22.930
<v Linda Lutton>Mr. Adams tells Jordan he needs to talk to him in his office. There have been changes in his schedule, he says, though this is just a ploy. They go to his office. And Mr. Adams gets to the real purpose. He asks Jordan where he lives.
<v Linda Lutton>亚当斯先生告诉乔丹他需要去办公室和他谈谈。他说他的课程表有变动，尽管这只是个借口。他们来到办公室，亚当斯先生进入了正题。他问乔丹住哪儿。

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:24.320
<v Jordan>69th and Loomis.
<v Jordan>第69街和卢米斯大道（Loomis）。

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:25.660
<v Chad Adams>69th and Loomis?
<v Chad Adams>第69街和卢米斯？

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:26.110
<v Jordan>Yeah.
<v Jordan>是的。

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:30.160
<v Chad Adams>So that way? Who's on that block?
<v Chad Adams>那么那边呢？那条街区里谁说了算？

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:30.640
<v Jordan>Like, what you mean?
<v Jordan>什么意思？

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:33.900
<v Chad Adams>You know. Who on that block? Who runs that block?
<v Chad Adams>你知道的。那条街区里谁在？谁在那儿掌权？

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:52.200
<v Linda Lutton>What Mr. Adams is trying to figure out is what gang Jordan is affiliated with and what gangs he might potentially have conflicts with here at Harper. Without hesitating, Jordan tells Mr. Adams he's affiliated with a gang called Face World. And they're friendly with a half dozen other gangs. They're cliqued up, kids say.
<v Linda Lutton>亚当斯先生试图弄清楚乔丹隶属于哪个帮派，以及他在哈珀可能会与哪些帮派发生潜在冲突。乔丹没有犹豫，告诉亚当斯先生他属于一个叫“Face World”的帮派。他们和其他六七个帮派关系友好。孩子们说，他们是“结盟”的。

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:55.000
<v Jordan>Well, we cliqued up with J-Town right there on 69th.
<v Jordan>我们和第69街那边的J-Town结盟了。

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.360
<v Chad Adams>Who else are y'all cliqued up with?
<v Chad Adams>你们还和谁结盟了？

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:10.450
<v Jordan>Hoodville, Low Block, Hit Squad, COB. We got a lot. A lot of people want to get into it with us, though, I ain't gonna lie.
<v Jordan>Hoodville, Low Block, Hit Squad, COB。我们有很多盟友。不过说实话，很多人想和我们过不去。

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:16.220
<v Chad Adams>I know. Your name was hot last spring. What was going on last spring?
<v Chad Adams>我知道。你的名字去年春天很火。去年春天发生了什么？

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:20.590
<v Jordan>It's a war zone around there. I can't lie. It's just a war zone.
<v Jordan>那附近是个战区。我不能撒谎。那就是个战区。

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:29.660
<v Jordan>People like us-- we so close to each other, it don't make no sense. 'Cause we right-- our opposition is right down the street. Literally, it's on the next block.
<v Jordan>像我们这样的人——离得太近了，这根本没意义。因为我们的对手就在街那头。真的，就在下一个街区。

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:37.100
<v Jordan>So, like, we on 70th and Rockwell, and they on 71st and Rockwell. That's how close we is. So--
<v Jordan>我们住在第70街和洛克威尔（Rockwell），他们住在第71街和洛克威尔。我们就这么近。所以——

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:55.710
<v Linda Lutton>They talk about this for 15 minutes. And what's remarkable is how matter of fact it is. They might as well be talking about what bus Jordan needs to take home or where the cafeteria is. There's no shame to admitting your gang affiliation. It's nothing you have to keep a secret. Mr. Adams has one more goal for this meeting.
<v Linda Lutton>他们谈论了15分钟。引人注目的是这谈话是多么地客观平淡。他们就像在谈论乔丹需要坐哪路公交车回家，或者食堂在哪里一样。承认自己的帮派背景并没有什么可耻的。这不是你需要保密的事情。亚当斯先生这次会议还有一个目标。

00:09:55.710 --> 00:10:02.230
<v Chad Adams>All right. So I haven't met you yet because you weren't here last year. But I'm Mr. Adams. I'm the assistant principal. This is my office.
<v Chad Adams>好了。我还没见过你，因为你去年不在这里。但我叫亚当斯先生。我是副校长。这是我的办公室。

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:16.310
<v Chad Adams>All right, so can we make an agreement today that if something happens in the block, or something happens in the school, that you'll-- that you'll come to me to help you fix the problem? And we'll use this?
<v Chad Adams>好的，我们今天能达成一个协议吗？如果街区里发生了什么，或者学校里发生了什么，你会——你会来找我，让我帮你解决问题？我们会动用这个？

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:18.620
<v Linda Lutton>Adams points to his head.
<v Linda Lutton>亚当斯指了指自己的头。

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:21.380
<v Chad Adams>Look at me, Mr. Rogers. We'll use this, instead of this.
<v Chad Adams>看着我，罗杰斯先生（Mr. Rogers）。我们要用这个，而不是用这个。

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:22.760
<v Linda Lutton>He points to his fists.
<v Linda Lutton>他指了指自己的拳头。

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:24.640
<v Chad Adams>Can we make an agreement?
<v Chad Adams>我们能达成协议吗？

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:34.430
<v Jordan>See, I make an agreement, like, if something happened in school. Like, if something happened in school, I would probably come to you and tell you, like, what was going down. But outside is a whole different story.
<v Jordan>看，如果学校里发生了什么事，我可以答应你，我会来告诉你发生了什么。但校外是完全不同的另一回事。

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:47.840
<v Chad Adams>And I'm not saying that I'm going to be able to help you with your problems outside the school. I'm just saying if something happens on the block that might lead back into the building, that you'll come to me so we can fix it here, so you don't have to worry about it here.
<v Chad Adams>我并不是说我能帮你解决校外的所有问题。我只是说，如果街区发生了可能蔓延到校内的事情，你会来找我，这样我们就能在这里解决，你就不必在这里担心它了。

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:55.040
<v Jordan>Um, I'll try to keep my worrying on the outside and might keep that agreement. But it is probably going to be hard, though.
<v Jordan>嗯，我会尽量把担心留在外面，可能会遵守那个协议。但那可能会很难。

00:10:55.040 --> 00:11:01.820
<v Chad Adams>OK. You know, and that's OK. I know it's going to be hard. It's not an easy agreement.
<v Chad Adams>好的。我知道这会很难。这不是一个轻松的协议。

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:27.190
<v Jordan>If it's-- if it's-- if I get into a problem with that [INAUDIBLE], if I get into a problem with one of [INAUDIBLE] people in this school, I ain't going to lie. I'm probably not even going to come here. I'll probably just go and do it right there, 'cause the problem escalated. 'Cause ain't no talking with them.
<v Jordan>如果——如果我遇到了那个[听不清]的问题，如果我遇到了学校里的某些人，我不会撒谎的。我可能甚至不会来这儿了。我可能就直接去那边解决掉，因为问题升级了。因为和他们没什么好谈的。

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:51.520
<v Linda Lutton>Maybe you think you have an idea of how street gangs operate. Crips and Bloods, people and folks, controlling huge swaths of the city, shooting it out over drug territory, a single gang leader controlling thousands of members, a strictly enforced hierarchy branching out underneath him, with gang colors and hats tilted to the right or left. For this hour, forget all that.
<v Linda Lutton>也许你以为你知道街头帮派是如何运作的。瘸子帮（Crips）和血帮（Bloods）、人民帮（People）和民间帮（Folks），控制着城市大片地区，为了毒品地盘火拼，单一的帮派头目控制着数千名成员，有着严格执行的等级制度，还要戴着向左或向右倾斜的帮派颜色帽子。接下来的这一小时，忘掉所有那些。

00:11:51.520 --> 00:12:04.870
<v Linda Lutton>The gangs in the Englewood neighborhood today are not those gangs. There's no central leader, no hierarchy, no colors. The fights aren't over drug territory. In fact, lots of these gangs aren't even selling drugs.
<v Linda Lutton>今天恩格尔伍德社区的帮派不是那些帮派。没有中央领导，没有等级制度，没有标志色。打架也不是为了毒品地盘。事实上，很多这样的帮派甚至不卖毒品。

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:18.170
<v Linda Lutton>They're different gangs with different rules. These rules apply absolutely to boys. Girls get slightly more leeway. Rule number one, look at a map.
<v Linda Lutton>它们是不同的帮派，有不同的规则。这些规则绝对适用于男孩。女孩则稍微宽松一点。规则一，看看地图。

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:33.920
<v Linda Lutton>When I ask kids what their parents don't understand about gangs these days, they say it's this. Their parents tell them not to join a gang, as if there's some initiation to go through, some way to sign up. Today, whether or not you want to be in a gang, you're in one.
<v Linda Lutton>当我问孩子们他们的父母对现在的帮派有什么不了解的地方时，他们说的是这个。他们的父母告诉他们不要加入帮派，好像那需要经过什么仪式，或者需要登记一样。今天，不管你想不想入伙，你都已经身在其中了。

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:47.300
<v Linda Lutton>If you live on pretty much any block near Harper High School, you have been assigned a gang. Your mother bought a house on 72nd and Hermitage? You're S Dub. You live across the street from the school? That's D-Ville.
<v Linda Lutton>如果你住在哈珀高中附近的任何一个街区，你就已经被分配了一个帮派。你妈妈在第72街和赫米蒂奇（Hermitage）买了一栋房子？你是S Dub帮的。你住在学校对面？那是D-Ville帮的。

00:12:47.300 --> 00:13:11.890
<v Linda Lutton>When you ask kids or cops or school staff how it got like this, they'll tell you that at one point this whole area was controlled pretty much by a single gang, the Gangster Disciples. But, and this is how most people tell this part of the story, Chicago police have been so effective at locking up the big gang leaders that the hierarchy of those gangs has crumbled. And that's left a lot of room for newcomers.
<v Linda Lutton>当你问孩子、警察或学校教职工它是如何变成这样的，他们会告诉你，一度这个地区基本上是由一个单一的帮派——“黑帮门徒”（Gangster Disciples）控制的。但是——这是大多数人讲述这部分故事的方式——芝加哥警方在逮捕大型帮派头目方面非常有效，导致这些帮派的等级制度崩溃了。这给新人留下了很大的空间。

00:13:11.890 --> 00:13:27.100
<v Linda Lutton>Your gang might control nothing more than the block you live on. In Harper's attendance area alone, which is a couple square miles, there are more than 15 gangs, also known as cliques, sets, factions, or crews. Some don't have anyone in charge.
<v Linda Lutton>你的帮派控制的可能只是你住的那个街区而已。仅在哈珀的生源区——几个平方英里——就有超过15个帮派，也被称为“小团体”（cliques）、“集合”（sets）、“派系”（factions）或“小队”（crews）。有些甚至没人负责。

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:35.540
<v Linda Lutton>But they do have guns. That's what every kid has told me. Otherwise, why would you call yourself a gang, they say.
<v Linda Lutton>但他们确实有枪。这是每个孩子告诉我的。他们说，不然你干嘛叫自己帮派？

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:50.620
<v Linda Lutton>Aaron Washington is a police officer assigned to Harper. He's there seven hours a day, seems to know every kid in the school. He says that for protection, for survival, kids walk to school with the kids in their clique, often through enemy territory.
<v Linda Lutton>亚伦·华盛顿（Aaron Washington）是一名分配到哈珀的警官。他每天在那里待七个小时，似乎认识学校里的每一个孩子。他说，为了保护自己，为了生存，孩子们会和他们小团体里的孩子一起步行去学校，经常穿过敌对地盘。

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:57.760
<v Linda Lutton>So I ask him, what if I'm a kid, and I really don't want any part of this gang stuff. How can I avoid it?
<v Linda Lutton>所以我问他，如果我是个孩子，我真的不想参与任何帮派的事情。我该怎么避开它？

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:01.960
<v Aaron Washington>You can't. It's not going to happen.
<v Aaron Washington>你避不开。那是不可能的。

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:06.600
<v Linda Lutton>He says it used to be possible to be neutral, what they called a neutron.
<v Linda Lutton>他说过去有可能保持中立，他们称之为“中子”（neutrons）。

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:24.160
<v Aaron Washington>There is no neutrons anymore. It used to be if you played sports or you were academically better than the average kid, they didn't bother you. Now it's different.
<v Aaron Washington>现在已经没有“中子”了。过去如果你参加体育运动或成绩比普通孩子好，他们就不会打扰你。现在不同了。

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:33.370
<v Aaron Washington>It doesn't matter. If you live here, you're part of them. You know, you live on that block, or you live in that area, you one of them.
<v Aaron Washington>没关系。如果你住在这里，你就是他们的一员。你知道，你住在那个街区，或者住在那个区域，你就是他们的人。

00:14:33.370 --> 00:14:47.330
<v Aaron Washington>The way they get to school, they have to come to school with one of these factions, one of these gangs. They going to come to school with them. They don't have a choice.
<v Aaron Washington>他们去学校的方式，他们必须和一个派系、一个帮派一起去学校。他们会和他们一起去学校。他们没得选择。

00:14:47.330 --> 00:15:06.280
<v Linda Lutton>I can hardly believe that a Chicago police officer is telling me this, admitting that kids don't have a choice about being gang affiliated. I've never heard police talk like this. Later I ask Officer Washington if he'll get in trouble for saying this. I mean, aren't cops supposed to just tell kids, hey, don't join a gang?
<v Linda Lutton>我几乎不敢相信一个芝加哥警察会告诉我这些，承认孩子们对是否加入帮派没得选择。我从未听过警察这样说话。后来我问华盛顿警官他这样说会不会惹麻烦。我是说，警察不应该只是告诉孩子，嘿，不要加入帮派吗？

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:16.000
<v Aaron Washington>I'll put it like this. I'm not saying it is OK to be in a gang. And I'm not saying I approve of it, I agree with it.
<v Aaron Washington>我这么说吧。我不是说加入帮派是可以的。我也不赞成，不认同它。

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:30.360
<v Aaron Washington>If I could take them all, and say, hey, look here, ain't no gangs, you know? I'd do that. But this ain't a fairy tale.
<v Aaron Washington>如果我能把他们都带走，说，嘿，听着，没有帮派，你知道吗？我会那样做。但这又不是童话故事。

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:44.180
<v Linda Lutton>And this is the point. Gangs aren't the bad kids in the corner here. They're the defining social structure in the school. It's who you sit with at lunch, the kids you say hi to in the hallway. It's the water everybody swims in.
<v Linda Lutton>重点就在这里。在这里，帮派不是角落里的坏孩子。它们是学校定义的社会结构。那是你午餐时坐在一起的人，是你在走廊里打招呼的孩子。这是每个人都在其中游动的“水”。

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:59.480
<v Linda Lutton>Assistant Principal Adams guesses that fewer than 10% of Harper students are actually gangbanging, that is, active on the block, involved in crime. He thinks all the rest of the kids in the school are just caught up by where they live.
<v Linda Lutton>副校长亚当斯估计，只有不到10%的哈珀学生在真正“混帮派”，即在街区活跃、参与犯罪。他认为学校里的其他孩子只是被他们居住的地方卷了进去。

00:15:59.480 --> 00:16:06.030
<v Linda Lutton>OK. So rule number one is know your geography. Rule number two, never walk by yourself.
<v Linda Lutton>好的，规则一是要了解你的地理位置。规则二，永远不要单独行走。

00:16:06.030 --> 00:16:07.880
<v Linda Lutton>[STREET NOISE]
<v Linda Lutton>[街道噪音]

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:12.040
<v Linda Lutton>One day at dismissal, I thought I saw a freshman walking home alone.
<v Linda Lutton>有一天放学时，我以为我看到一个一年级新生独自走回家。

00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:14.720
<v Linda Lutton>I stopped you because you were walking by yourself.
<v Linda Lutton>我叫住你是为了因为你一个人在走。

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:15.820
<v Ira Glass>But I was wrong.
<v Ira Glass>但我错了。

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:17.030
<v Larnell>We're walking with them.
<v Larnell>我们正和他们一起走。

00:16:17.030 --> 00:16:21.110
<v Linda Lutton>Larnell now pointed over his shoulder at a couple of girls about 15 feet back.
<v Linda Lutton>拉内尔（Larnell）指了指身后大约15英尺远的一对女孩。

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:23.870
<v Linda Lutton>So you're actually walking with the girls back there?
<v Linda Lutton>所以你其实是和那后面的女孩们一起走的？

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:26.680
<v Larnell>Yeah, I always walk with people home.
<v Larnell>对，我总是和人一起回家。

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:28.660
<v Linda Lutton>What's the advantage?
<v Linda Lutton>有什么好处吗？

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:38.250
<v Larnell>It's not trying to get jumped on and shot. 'Cause they be fighting and shooting up there almost every day. Because won't nobody mess with somebody in a group, walking in a group.
<v Larnell>为了不被袭击和枪击。因为他们几乎每天都在那儿打架和开枪。因为没人会去招惹一群一起走的人。

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:45.950
<v Linda Lutton>And that's true. But it's complicated, because of rule number three. Rule number three, never walk with someone else.
<v Linda Lutton>这是真的。但这很复杂，因为有规则三。规则三，永远不要和别人一起走。

00:16:45.950 --> 00:17:09.510
<v Linda Lutton>See, walking in a group can send its own message. If you're with a group of boys in Englewood-- on your porch, walking home from school-- you're highlighting your affiliation, which makes you more of a target. It's a huge catch-22 for kids in this neighborhood. If you walk alone, you risk being jumped. If you walk with someone else, you risk being labeled as a gang member and being shot.
<v Linda Lutton>你看，一群人一起走也会传达一种信息。如果你在恩格尔伍德的一群男孩中——在你的门廊上，从学校回家——你就是在凸显你的所属关系，这让你成为了更大的目标。这对这个街区的孩子来说是一个巨大的“第22条军规”（进退两难）。如果你单独走，你冒着被袭击的风险。如果你和别人一起走，你冒着被贴上帮派成员标签并被枪击的风险。

00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:22.230
<v Linda Lutton>Rule number four, don't use the sidewalk. Every day at dismissal, kids drift out of Harper High School and walk along Wood Street, actually, right down the middle of Wood Street. It's a strange scene.
<v Linda Lutton>规则四，不要走人行道。每天放学时，孩子们从哈珀高中出来，沿着伍德街（Wood Street）行走，实际上，就走在伍德街的正中央。这是一个奇怪的场景。

00:17:22.230 --> 00:17:39.090
<v Linda Lutton>Cars drive slowly, waiting for students to move out of the way. One teacher told me that when she first arrived at Harper, she thought this was just plain hooliganism, the teenagers taking over. One afternoon, a girl named Alex explained, that's not it at all.
<v Linda Lutton>汽车缓慢行驶，等待学生们让路。一位老师告诉我，她刚到哈珀时，认为这简直是流氓行为，青少年们占领了街道。一天下午，一个叫亚历克斯（Alex）的女孩解释说，根本不是那回事。

00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:48.990
<v Alex>We feel safer like this. For some reason, we just feel safe like that. We never like to walk past trees and stuff. There's too much stuff going on.
<v Alex>我们这样觉得更安全。出于某种原因，我们只是觉得那样更安全。我们从不喜欢走过树丛什么的。那里发生的事情太多了。

00:17:48.990 --> 00:18:07.560
<v Linda Lutton>Too much stuff going on is shorthand here for the shootings, the fights, the craziness. It's better to walk down the middle of the street, where you can keep a broad view of things, and where you have a few more seconds to run if you need to. Rule number five, if they shoot, don't run.
<v Linda Lutton>“发生的事情太多了”在这里是对枪击、打架和疯狂事件的委婉说法。最好走在街道中央，在那里你可以保持广阔的视野，而且如果你需要跑，你会有几秒钟的时间。规则五，如果他们开枪，不要跑。

00:18:07.560 --> 00:18:24.300
<v Linda Lutton>12th grader Antoryio was on the Harper High School football team. In fact, he's one of the best running backs in the entire city of Chicago. On the field, he zips around linemen like they're not even there, cutting and weaving, and then racing for the end zone. Those are skills he purposefully ignores when shot at.
<v Linda Lutton>12年级的安托里奥（Antoryio）是哈珀高中橄榄球队的成员。事实上，他是整个芝加哥最好的跑卫之一。在赛场上，他绕过防守队员，仿佛他们根本不存在，切割、穿梭，然后直冲终点区。当被开枪射击时，他刻意忽略了这些技巧。

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:26.830
<v Antoryio Barton>I fall to the ground.
<v Antoryio Barton>我趴在地上。

00:18:26.830 --> 00:18:28.510
<v Linda Lutton>That's your strategy?
<v Linda Lutton>那是你的策略？

00:18:28.510 --> 00:18:44.490
<v Antoryio Barton>Yeah, 'cause you run, you'll probably get shot in the back or something like that. So I just fall to the ground. Most people, like, shoot from, like-- say, we, like, in front of my house, they'd shoot from the corner. Or do a drive-by in their cars, so I just fall to the ground.
<v Antoryio Barton>对，因为如果你跑，你很可能会背部中弹什么的。所以我只是趴在地上。大多数人，就像——说吧，我们在我家门前，他们会从拐角处开枪。或者在车里开车枪击，所以我只是趴在地上。

00:18:44.490 --> 00:18:58.510
<v Linda Lutton>OK. By now you may be wondering, if these gangs aren't fighting over drug territory, what are the shootings about? That brings us to rule number six. Rule number six, you can be shot for reasons big and small.
<v Linda Lutton>好的。到现在你可能在想，如果这些帮派不是为了毒品地盘而战，那枪击是为了什么？这让我们想到了规则六。规则六，你可能因为大事小事被枪杀。

00:18:58.510 --> 00:19:17.010
<v Linda Lutton>If you ask the police or school officials or kids what the shootings are about, they'll mention girls, money owed. There was a paintball incident that led to real guns going off. Petty stuff, like losing a fist fight, he-said she-said arguments, often they'll tell you a shooting is over nothing.
<v Linda Lutton>如果你问警察、学校官员或孩子们枪击是为了什么，他们会提到女孩、欠款。有一个彩弹枪事件导致了真正的枪支走火。琐碎的事情，比如输了一场拳架，口角争执，他们经常告诉你枪击是为了“没什么”。

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:28.690
<v Linda Lutton>Retaliation for earlier shootings is a big reason for getting shot. Shootings can ping pong back and forth between rival gangs for years. Of course, you can also be shot for walking off your block.
<v Linda Lutton>为了之前的枪击而报复是中枪的一个大原因。枪击可以在敌对帮派之间反复发生，持续多年。当然，你也会因为离开了你的街区而被枪杀。

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:47.030
<v Linda Lutton>And finally, rule number seven, never go outside. When I ask kids for advice about staying alive in this neighborhood, they told me the best advice was to stay away from your block as long as possible every day. Get involved in something at school so you can stay as late as they let you.
<v Linda Lutton>最后，规则七，永远不要去外面。当我问孩子们关于在这个街区保持活命的建议时，他们告诉我最好的建议是每天尽可能远离你的街区。参加学校里的活动，这样你就可以尽量待到很晚。

00:19:47.030 --> 00:20:01.640
<v Linda Lutton>When you do go home, don't leave the house. Don't even go on the porch. If you want to see the lengths you have to go to not be part of the gang, you should meet a senior named Deonte. Being anti-gang is Deonte's entire identity.
<v Linda Lutton>当你确实回家时，不要离开房子。甚至不要去门廊。如果你想看看为了不成为帮派一部分所必须采取的手段，你应该见见一个叫德翁特（Deonte）的高年级学生。反对帮派是德翁特身份的全部。

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:17.210
<v Linda Lutton>He's an outspoken Christian. He holds Bible study in his living room. Other kids come to him for advice, a role he wholly embraces. He's poised to be the valedictorian. When you talk to Deonte, you get a sense of what it takes to stay away from the gangs.
<v Linda Lutton>他是一位直言不讳的基督徒。他在客厅里举办圣经学习班。其他孩子来向他寻求建议，他完全接受了这个角色。他有望成为毕业典礼演讲人。当你和德翁特交谈时，你会感受到远离帮派需要付出什么。

00:20:17.210 --> 00:20:20.390
<v Linda Lutton>Do you ever go out, like just around the neighborhood?
<v Linda Lutton>你出去过吗，就在街区附近？

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:39.920
<v Deonte>Oh, no. Not at all, and in a way, that can be bad as well. Because that's when depression is easy to set in. That took a hold on me, 'cause I've been in the house for about three years. I've been staying in the house a lot.
<v Deonte>哦，不。一点也没有，而且在某种程度上，那也可能很糟糕。因为那是抑郁症最容易侵入的时候。这对我产生了影响，因为我已经在家里待了大约三年了。我大部分时间都待在家里。

00:20:39.920 --> 00:20:42.440
<v Linda Lutton>Do you feel lonely?
<v Linda Lutton>你感到孤独吗？

00:20:42.440 --> 00:20:55.170
<v Deonte>At times. At times, I feel lonely. At times, I would want to have some friends. 'Cause I'm not really friends with anybody.
<v Deonte>有时。有时，我感到孤独。有时，我想要一些朋友。因为我和任何人都不算是真正的朋友。

00:20:55.170 --> 00:21:21.500
<v Linda Lutton>If you think about high school, how important friends are during that time, imagine going through that with your whole goal being to avoid your school's social structure completely for four years. It's an incredibly high price to steer clear of the violence. It's a price most teenagers anywhere would find almost impossible to pay.
<v Linda Lutton>如果你想想高中，那个时期朋友有多重要，想象一下，带着整个目标——即在四年里完全避开学校的社会结构——来度过这段时期。为了远离暴力，这付出了极其高昂的代价。这是大多数青少年在任何地方都会发现几乎无法支付的代价。

00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:42.720
<v Ira Glass>Linda Lutton. Act Two, a Tiny Office on the Second Floor. So the place in the school where staff deals most directly with the effects of the violence on students is the social work office, as you'd kind of expect. Alex Kotlowitz has this story about one of the students that the social workers took under their wings.
<v Ira Glass>琳达·勒顿（Linda Lutton）。第二幕，二楼的一个小办公室。所以，学校里处理暴力对学生影响最直接的地方是社会工作办公室，正如你所预料的那样。亚历克斯·科特洛维茨（Alex Kotlowitz）讲了这个故事，关于社会工作者庇护下的一名学生。

00:21:42.720 --> 00:22:03.410
<v Alex Kotlowitz>I first met Devonte, a junior, back on the first day of school when he and his mom came in for a meeting with Principal Sanders. Devonte was a Harper student who had been temporarily transferred and was now just returning. Sanders was happy to see him, but everyone seemed a little tense, a little careful. I couldn't tell exactly what was going on.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>我第一次见到德翁特（Devonte），一个高二学生，是在开学第一天，当时他和妈妈来参加与桑德斯校长的会议。德翁特曾是一名暂时转学的哈珀学生，现在正准备回来。桑德斯很高兴见到他，但每个人看起来都有点紧张，有点小心翼翼。我无法准确判断发生了什么。

00:22:03.410 --> 00:22:14.070
<v Leonetta Sanders>And we're not going to push you to get into any activities. Just, you know, you take your time right now. Just get back into the swing of school. 'Cause we starting a whole fresh slate here.
<v Leonetta Sanders>我们不会强迫你参加任何活动。只要，你知道，现在花点时间。只要重新适应学校的节奏。因为我们在这里开启了一个全新的开始。

00:22:14.070 --> 00:22:30.270
<v Alex Kotlowitz>It was only later that I learned the full story. That last February, Devonte accidentally shot and killed his 14-year-old brother. After the shooting, the Harper staff had been concerned that kids might taunt Devonte, so he was transferred to a different school.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>直到后来我才得知完整的故事。去年二月，德翁特意外开枪打死了他14岁的弟弟。枪击事件后，哈珀的教职工担心孩子们可能会嘲讽德翁特，所以他被转到了另一所学校。

00:22:30.270 --> 00:22:40.130
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Now that he was back, a few weeks into school, he's been stopping by the school social worker's office two or three times a day to meet with one of the social workers there, Crystal Smith.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>现在他回来了，开学几周后，他每天两三次到学校社会工作者的办公室，与其中一名社会工作者克里斯托·史密斯（Crystal Smith）会面。

00:22:40.130 --> 00:22:48.730
<v Crystal Smith>Close that door for me, [INAUDIBLE]. Because you know, I don't what people, you know, walking by, all up in our conversation and stuff, you know?
<v Crystal Smith>帮我关上那扇门，[听不清]。因为你知道，我不想让人，你知道，走来走去，插嘴我们的谈话什么的，你知道吗？

00:22:48.730 --> 00:22:49.460
<v Devonte>Mhm.
<v Devonte>嗯。

00:22:49.460 --> 00:22:51.460
<v Crystal Smith>Is that OK?
<v Crystal Smith>可以吗？

00:22:51.460 --> 00:23:07.270
<v Alex Kotlowitz>The shooting happened last winter, after school. Devonte says his brother had somehow gotten his hands on an old handgun, which neither of them thought worked. In their third floor apartment, they both handled the weapon. While Devonte was holding it, it somehow went off.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>枪击发生在去年冬天放学后。德翁特说他的弟弟以某种方式弄到了一把旧手枪，他们俩都以为它不能用。在三楼的公寓里，他们都拿了那把武器。当德翁特握着它时，它不知怎么就走火了。

00:23:07.270 --> 00:23:24.250
<v Alex Kotlowitz>A judge later ruled it an accident-- reckless discharge of a firearm. Devonte's brother Damion died an hour later in the hospital. Devonte hasn't had any intensive counseling since the shooting. So for Crystal, establishing a relationship with him is tricky.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>法官后来裁定这是一起意外——鲁莽开枪。德翁特的弟弟达米恩（Damion）一小时后在医院去世。枪击事件后，德翁特没有接受过任何密集的心理辅导。所以对于克里斯托来说，与他建立关系很棘手。

00:23:24.250 --> 00:23:27.720
<v Crystal Smith>What you eat for lunch? How you going to not let me share it with you?
<v Crystal Smith>你午饭吃了什么？你打算怎么不让我和你分享？

00:23:27.720 --> 00:23:30.070
<v Devonte>I ate, um, chicken patties.
<v Devonte>我吃了，嗯，鸡肉饼。

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:49.270
<v Alex Kotlowitz>To make him feel safe enough with her to talk about his feelings, and most importantly about his guilt, she often bounces around in their meetings from totally superficial things, like lunch, to more substantive subjects, like Devonte smoking marijuana. He tells Crystal he hasn't smoked in a while. And she does an impression of him when he's high.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>为了让他感到足够安全可以和她谈论自己的感受，尤其是关于他的内疚，她在会议中经常从完全肤浅的事情（比如午餐）跳跃到更实质性的主题（比如德翁特抽大麻）。他告诉克里斯托他已经有一段时间没抽了。她模仿了他吸大麻后的样子。

00:23:49.270 --> 00:24:04.500
<v Crystal Smith>When you're smoking, you be, like, (SLURRING) what's up, Miss Smith? I'd be like, dude, you know you high, right? (SLURRING) Naw, I'm straight. [LAUGHS] That'd be you. You don't even be remembering that we done had a conversation, do you?
<v Crystal Smith>当你抽的时候，你就像，（口齿不清）嘿，史密斯小姐？我会说，伙计，你知道你嗨了吧，对吧？（口齿不清）不，我很清醒。[笑声]那会是你。你甚至都不记得我们刚才谈过话了，是吧？

00:24:04.500 --> 00:24:05.060
<v Devonte>No.
<v Devonte>不。

00:24:05.060 --> 00:24:06.520
<v Crystal Smith>It's OK, though.
<v Crystal Smith>没关系。

00:24:06.520 --> 00:24:09.360
<v Devonte>I know-- I do, I do remember this one, though.
<v Devonte>我知道——但我确实记得这一件。

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:11.170
<v Crystal Smith>What?
<v Crystal Smith>什么？

00:24:11.170 --> 00:24:15.490
<v Devonte>To my moms, who might throw my little brother's bed away, and I don't want her to throw it away and stuff.
<v Devonte>对我妈，她可能会把小弟弟的床扔掉，我不希望她把它扔掉什么的。

00:24:15.490 --> 00:24:19.270
<v Alex Kotlowitz>He's worried his mom might throw away his little brother's bed.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>他担心他妈妈可能会扔掉他弟弟的床。

00:24:19.270 --> 00:24:43.360
<v Crystal Smith>So we talked about-- can I say this? It helps him to sleep at night if he sleeps in his brother's bed. But his mother was contemplating throwing the bed away. And so I had told him that if I needed to, I would call her and let her know why it's important for him to be able to, you know, keep the bed. Right?
<v Crystal Smith>所以我们谈到了——我可以这么说吗？如果他睡在他弟弟的床上，这有助于他在晚上睡觉。但他母亲正在考虑把那张床扔掉。所以我告诉他，如果有必要，我会给她打电话，让她知道为什么他能保留那张床很重要。对吧？

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:44.340
<v Devonte>Mhm.
<v Devonte>嗯。

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:45.610
<v Crystal Smith>You still with me?
<v Crystal Smith>你还在听吗？

00:24:45.610 --> 00:24:46.090
<v Devonte>Yeah, I'm with you.
<v Devonte>是的，我在听。

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:48.850
<v Crystal Smith>If it's too much, you just say, I'm done.
<v Crystal Smith>如果太沉重了，你就说，我受不了了。

00:24:48.850 --> 00:24:49.990
<v Devonte>Nah, it ain't too much.
<v Devonte>不，那不沉重。

00:24:49.990 --> 00:24:52.220
<v Crystal Smith>OK.
<v Crystal Smith>好的。

00:24:52.220 --> 00:25:11.070
<v Alex Kotlowitz>The day after his brother's funeral, Devonte showed up at Harper wearing a t-shirt with a picture of his brother on it. There were food stains on the shirt, leading Crystal to believe he'd probably slept in it. Crystal says before the shooting, Devonte barely came to school. He was aggressive and surly with the staff.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>在他弟弟葬礼后的第二天，德翁特穿着一件印有他弟弟照片的T恤来到哈珀。衬衫上有食物污渍，导致克里斯托认为他可能穿着它睡了觉。克里斯托说在枪击事件前，德翁特几乎不来学校。他对教职工很暴躁。

00:25:11.070 --> 00:25:34.910
<v Alex Kotlowitz>But here he was, the day after burying his brother. And Crystal realized that Devonte needed to be at Harper, that he needed her. She's still just at the beginning of figuring out what she can do for Devonte. He's cracked the door, she told me, but it's the size of a mouse hole.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>但就在这里，在他安葬弟弟后的第二天。克里斯托意识到德翁特需要哈珀，他需要她。她现在只是刚开始搞清楚她能为德翁特做什么。他已经打开了那扇门，她告诉我，但它只有老鼠洞那么大。

00:25:34.910 --> 00:26:00.860
<v Alex Kotlowitz>I spent most of my time at Harper in the social work office. Crystal has a caseload of 55 kids, each of whom struggles with a learning disability or some kind of emotional issue. Another part-time social worker and a school psychologist also have a caseload of kids. But they make strong personal connections with lots of Harper students, not just those they're assigned to. And more often than not, because of some violent incident.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>我在哈珀的大部分时间都在社会工作办公室。克里斯托负责55个孩子，每个孩子都有学习障碍或某种情绪问题。另一位兼职社会工作者和一名学校心理学家也负责一批孩子。但他们与许多哈珀学生建立了深厚的个人联系，不仅仅是他们负责的那些。而且往往是因为某种暴力事件。

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:10.900
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Harper, of course, has just come off this horribly violent year. And I guess the question I had, and honestly, the question that the social workers ask themselves is, can they make a difference?
<v Alex Kotlowitz>当然，哈珀刚刚经历了这一极度暴力的一年。我想我有一个问题，老实说，也是社会工作者问自己的问题是，他们能带来改变吗？

00:26:10.900 --> 00:26:14.590
<v Alex Kotlowitz>[SCHOOL HALL CHATTER]
<v Alex Kotlowitz>[学校走廊的嘈杂声]

00:26:14.590 --> 00:26:20.350
<v Crystal Smith>Hello! How are you? [INAUDIBLE]. Hi, Shari!
<v Crystal Smith>你好！你好吗？[听不清]。嗨，莎莉！

00:26:20.350 --> 00:26:25.590
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Crystal is in the halls for every passing period with a constant patter of positivity.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>克里斯托在每个课间休息时间都在走廊里，不断散发着积极向上的氛围。

00:26:25.590 --> 00:26:34.480
<v Crystal Smith>I'm so proud of you. I see you trying hard. Keep it up, OK? I'm so proud of you! Go, go, go! So you won't be late. Go, go, go! Y'all moving around, right?
<v Crystal Smith>我为你感到骄傲。我看到你很努力。继续保持，好吗？我为你感到骄傲！走吧，走吧，走吧！这样你才不会迟到。走吧，走吧，走吧！你们都在动起来，对吧？

00:26:34.480 --> 00:26:34.960
<v Student>Yeah.
<v Student>对。

00:26:34.960 --> 00:26:38.570
<v Crystal Smith>Thank you so much. Let me appreciate you in advance.
<v Crystal Smith>非常感谢。让我预先感谢你。

00:26:38.570 --> 00:26:44.710
<v Alex Kotlowitz>I appreciate you in advance, a phrase I've never heard anyone use before, is one of Crystal's trademarks.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>“我预先感谢你”，这句我从未听过别人使用的话，是克里斯托的招牌之一。

00:26:44.710 --> 00:26:46.420
<v Crystal Smith>Do you have your headphones in while you're talking to me?
<v Crystal Smith>你在和我说话时戴着耳机吗？

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:47.470
<v Terrell>I'm not-- it's low.
<v Terrell>我没——声音很小。

00:26:47.470 --> 00:26:50.110
<v Crystal Smith>Is it off? Could you take them out so I won't feel like I'm being disrespected?
<v Crystal Smith>关了吗？你能取下来吗，这样我就不会觉得我不被尊重了？

00:26:50.110 --> 00:26:51.120
<v Terrell>I'm not.
<v Terrell>我没戴。

00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:51.820
<v Crystal Smith>Thank you so much.
<v Crystal Smith>非常感谢。

00:26:51.820 --> 00:26:52.180
<v Terrell>It ain't nothing playing.
<v Terrell>什么都没在放。

00:26:52.180 --> 00:26:54.890
<v Crystal Smith>I appreciate you in advance. Terrell, how was your summer?
<v Crystal Smith>我预先感谢你。特雷尔（Terrell），你暑假过得怎么样？

00:26:54.890 --> 00:26:57.030
<v Terrell>It was OK, safe.
<v Terrell>还行，安全。

00:26:57.030 --> 00:26:57.360
<v Crystal Smith>Safe?
<v Crystal Smith>安全？

00:26:57.360 --> 00:26:58.790
<v Terrell>Yeah.
<v Terrell>对。

00:26:58.790 --> 00:27:08.400
<v Crystal Smith>That's great. You're the only person that said that to me. That means something to me. I'm glad it was safe. Good morning, gentlemen. Good morning.
<v Crystal Smith>太棒了。你是唯一一个这么对我说的。这对我意义重大。我很高兴它是安全的。早上好，先生们。早上好。

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:31.170
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Even when a kid characterizes his summer not as fun or relaxing, but as safe, Crystal sees the bright side. I've seen her practically tackle students to tell them, you're a valuable person. You matter. When she's doling out advice, some of the kids laughingly call it Mom Patrol. Sometimes, though, I wonder if the kids see past her perkiness. Because she's really a force.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>即使当一个孩子形容他的夏天不是有趣或放松，而是“安全”时，克里斯托也能看到光明的一面。我见过她几乎扑上去告诉学生：你是一个有价值的人。你很重要。当她分发建议时，一些孩子笑着称之为“妈妈巡逻队”。然而，有时我想知道孩子们是否看穿了她的活泼。因为她确实是一股力量。

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:38.610
<v Crystal Smith>I thank you for being to class on time. Thank you! I love you!
<v Crystal Smith>我感谢你们准时上课。谢谢！我爱你们！

00:27:38.610 --> 00:27:39.110
<v Crystal Smith>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Crystal Smith>[不同声音交叉]

00:27:39.110 --> 00:27:40.950
<v Student>I'm up out of here. I'm fixing to go to class.
<v Student>我要离开这里了。我正准备去上课。

00:27:40.950 --> 00:27:59.930
<v Alex Kotlowitz>It's six weeks later, late September, and the social work office is packed. All times of day, kids pile into the social work office, a crowded, windowless room stuffed with three desks, a filing cabinet, and a paper shredder. So many kids that at times you have to jostle for a place to stand.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>六周后，九月下旬，社会工作办公室挤满了人。一天中的所有时间，孩子们都涌入社会工作办公室，一个拥挤、没有窗户、塞着三张桌子、一个档案柜和一个碎纸机的房间。人多的时候，你不得不为站立的地方挤来挤去。

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:13.340
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Some of the kids come because their educational plans mandate social work visits. And others come just because they like talking with Crystal and Anita Stewart, the school's other social worker. Devonte is often in the office.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>有些孩子来是因为他们的教育计划强制要求社会工作访问。其他人来只是因为他们喜欢和克里斯托以及安妮塔·斯图尔特（Anita Stewart，学校的另一位社会工作者）交谈。德翁特经常在办公室。

00:28:13.340 --> 00:28:30.320
<v Alex Kotlowitz>Before the incident last February where Devonte accidentally shot his 14-year-old brother, he didn't have much of a relationship with Crystal or Anita. But now, they're really getting close. Earlier in the year, Devonte had told them he wasn't sleeping well. And today, Anita and Crystal want to talk about it.
<v Alex Kotlowitz>在去年二月德翁特意外枪杀他14岁弟弟的事件之前，他与克里斯托或安妮塔没有多少交际。但现在，他们真的变得亲密了。年初，德翁特告诉他们他睡得不好。今天，安妮塔和克里斯托想谈谈这个。

00:28:30.320 --> 00:28:33.360
<v Anita Stewart>So what about sleeping and all of that, Devonte? Are you sleeping OK?
<v Anita Stewart>那么睡觉什么的怎么样，德翁特？你睡得还好吗？

00:28:33.360 --> 00:28:34.690
<v Devonte>Yeah.
<v Devonte>嗯。

00:28:34.690 --> 00:28:37.310
<v Crystal Smith>What you do at night to help you though?
<v Crystal Smith>你晚上做了什么来帮助你？

00:28:37.310 --> 00:28:40.550
<v Devonte>To go to sleep? I take some Nyquil.
<v Devonte>睡觉？我喝点尼奎尔（Nyquil）。

00:28:40.550 --> 00:28:41.910
<v Crystal Smith>Oh, you still taking Nyquil?
<v Crystal Smith>哦，你还在喝尼奎尔？

00:28:41.910 --> 00:28:46.750
<v Devonte>Man, I take it on a regular base. 'Cause I need to go to sleep, man.
<v Devonte>伙计，我经常喝。因为我需要睡觉，伙计。

00:28:46.750 --> 00:28:48.230
<v Crystal Smith>So we need to talk about--
<v Crystal Smith>所以我们需要谈谈——

00:28:48.230 --> 00:28:49.390
<v Devonte>I need to sleep tight.
<v Devonte>我需要睡个好觉。

00:28:49.390 --> 00:28:50.950
<v Anita Stewart>What happens if you don't take the Nyquil?
<v Anita Stewart>如果你不喝尼奎尔会怎样？

00:28:50.950 --> 00:28:51.870
<v Anita Stewart>[TELEPHONE RINGING]
<v Anita Stewart>[电话铃声]

00:28:51.870 --> 00:28:54.480
<v Devonte>I don't go to sleep.
<v Devonte>我睡不着。

00:28:54.480 --> 00:29:00.210
<v Crystal Smith>Can I ask you a question? What are the thoughts that you have when you get ready to go to sleep?
<v Crystal Smith>我能问你一个问题吗？当你准备睡觉时，你会有什么想法？

00:29:00.210 --> 00:29:01.700
<v Devonte>None.
<v Devonte>没有。

00:29:01.700 --> 00:29:06.500
<v Crystal Smith>You having some kind thoughts that you're trying to escape. So that that way--
<v Crystal Smith>你有一些你试图逃避的想法。所以那样的话——

00:29:06.500 --> 00:29:07.670
<v Devonte>I just be up thinking about stuff.
<v Devonte>我只是躺着想事情。

00:29:07.670 --> 00:29:10.860
<v Crystal Smith>What? One thought that you be thinking about?
