<v Ira Glass>About half the kids on the team are affiliated with one gang. There are five or six other gangs represented on the team, some of them opposition. But football actually seems to be the one place at Harper where everybody truly puts those rivalries aside. Ben Calhoun was at Harper for homecoming week.
<v Ira Glass>队里大约一半的孩子与一个帮派有关。队里还有五六个其他帮派的代表，其中一些是敌对的。但橄榄球实际上似乎是哈珀唯一一个每个人真正能把那些竞争放在一边的地方。本·卡尔霍恩（Ben Calhoun）在哈珀报道返校周。

00:39:13.930 --> 00:39:30.640
<v Ben Calhoun>At this point in the school year, Harper had been pretty violence free. There had been some fights, some big ones. But week after week was going by without a shooting. This was something that people, especially staff, they talked about it in this careful way, like they didn't want to jinx it.
<v Ben Calhoun>在学年的这个阶段，哈珀几乎没有暴力事件。发生过一些打架，有些还是大的。但一周又一周过去了，没有发生枪击。这是一个人们，特别是教职工谈论时小心翼翼的事，好像他们不想给它带来厄运一样。

00:39:30.640 --> 00:39:47.350
<v Ben Calhoun>The big events of homecoming week, the dance and the game, were on Friday. Thursday afternoon, every student's in the school's bleachers for a pep rally-- cheerleaders, DJ in the front, playing songs that the students dance to, and the staff occasionally feels uncomfortable to.
<v Ben Calhoun>返校周的盛大活动，舞会和比赛，在周五。周四下午，每个学生都在学校看台上参加动员会——啦啦队、DJ在前面，播放学生们跳舞的歌曲，教职工偶尔也会感到不自在。

00:39:47.350 --> 00:39:48.050
<v Ben Calhoun>[MUSIC - T-PAIN, "BOOTY WURK"]
<v Ben Calhoun>[音乐 - T-PAIN, "BOOTY WURK"]

00:39:48.050 --> 00:39:53.240
<v Ben Calhoun>(RAPPING) Now let me see that booty work, booty work, booty boo boo booty work. Booty work.
<v Ben Calhoun>（说唱）现在让我看看那个屁股运动，屁股运动，屁股屁股运动。屁股运动。

00:39:53.240 --> 00:40:02.730
<v Ira Glass>This season, Harper's not just undefeated. They've been demolishing other schools-- scores like 46 to 0, 47 to 0. They're good, really good.
<v Ira Glass>本赛季，哈珀不仅不败。他们还横扫了其他学校——比分比如46比0，47比0。他们很棒，真的非常棒。

00:40:02.730 --> 00:40:03.530
<v Woman>All right! All right!
<v Woman>好的！好的！

00:40:03.530 --> 00:40:16.820
<v Ben Calhoun>Last year, Harper went to the Chicago Public school championship, a real feat for a school that's so tiny. They were up against a Chicago sports Goliath, Simeon High School, a school known for producing professional athletes.
<v Ben Calhoun>去年，哈珀进入了芝加哥公立学校锦标赛，这对一所如此小的学校来说是一个真正的壮举。他们面对的是芝加哥体育巨人，西蒙高中（Simeon High School），一所以培养职业运动员而闻名的学校。

00:40:16.820 --> 00:40:33.460
<v Ben Calhoun>The day of the championship, Simeon had four times as many players suited up for the game. Harper's roster is so small just about everybody has to play both offense and defense. Harper lost that game. But they came so close, people are psyched for a second chance.
<v Ben Calhoun>锦标赛当天，西蒙队穿戴整齐参赛的球员数量是哈珀的四倍。哈珀的名单太小了，几乎每个人都必须同时打进攻和防守。哈珀输掉了那场比赛。但他们如此接近，人们对第二次机会感到兴奋。

00:40:33.460 --> 00:40:38.530
<v Pep Rally Announcer 1>And we know the football team tomorrow is going to whoop a little butt on Marshall's!
<v Pep Rally Announcer 1>我们知道橄榄球队明天会把马歇尔高中打得落花流水！

00:40:38.530 --> 00:40:41.010
<v Pep Rally Announcer 1>[CHEERING]
<v Pep Rally Announcer 1>[欢呼声]

00:40:41.010 --> 00:40:49.270
<v Ben Calhoun>The team runs in one player at a time, and eventually, they get to a senior, a popular kid named Damoni Ware, who goes by the nickname, "Money."
<v Ben Calhoun>球队一个接一个地跑入，最后，他们轮到了高三生，一个叫达莫尼·威尔（Damoni Ware）的受欢迎的孩子，他的绰号是“钱”（Money）。

00:40:49.270 --> 00:40:50.930
<v Pep Rally Announcer 1>Number 2, Damoni Ware!
<v Pep Rally Announcer 1>2号，达莫尼·威尔！

00:40:50.930 --> 00:40:54.680
<v Pep Rally Announcer 1>[CHEERING]
<v Pep Rally Announcer 1>[欢呼声]

00:40:54.680 --> 00:41:08.320
<v Ben Calhoun>And Damoni runs out, like everybody else, like a high school kid on a good day. Then just a couple of minutes later, Money's walking out to the front again. This time, it's because he's one of four candidates for Homecoming King.
<v Ben Calhoun>达莫尼像其他人一样跑出来，就像个好日子里的高中生。然后就在几分钟后，“钱”又向前面走去。这次，是因为他是四名返校节国王候选人之一。

00:41:08.320 --> 00:41:13.960
<v Pep Rally Announcer 2>Damoni Ware and Joshua Meyer!
<v Pep Rally Announcer 2>达莫尼·威尔和约书亚·迈耶（Joshua Meyer）！

00:41:13.960 --> 00:41:32.710
<v Damoni Ware>Josh was walking up that way. I mean, he was a contestant for Homecoming King. And when I was walking up there, they call his name right after mine. And he told me what happened, like, he was nice to me. He said it out loud. He wasn't trying to whisper it or nothing. He just said it in his regular tone of voice. He said, Little James just got shot.
<v Damoni Ware>乔什正往那边走。我的意思是，他是返校节国王的选手。当我走在那儿时，他们就在我的名字之后叫了他的名字。他告诉我发生了什么，就像，他对我很友好。他大声说出来了。他没打算耳语或者什么。他只是用正常的语调说的。他说，“小詹姆斯刚刚中枪了。”

00:41:32.710 --> 00:41:52.990
<v Ben Calhoun>Money told us about this later. About how, at the rally, as he stood there with his friend, Josh, Josh was on the phone relaying news about another friend of theirs named James Williams. James had been shot just a few blocks from Harper, meaning probably about the time Money was running under the cheerleaders pompoms, someone was shooting at his friend.
<v Ben Calhoun>“钱”后来告诉了我们这件事。关于在动员会上，当他和朋友乔什站在一起时，乔什正在电话里转达关于他们另一个叫詹姆斯·威廉姆斯（James Williams）的朋友的消息。詹姆斯就在离哈珀几个街区的地方中枪了，这意味着可能在“钱”跑过啦啦队彩球的时候，有人正朝他的朋友开枪。

00:41:52.990 --> 00:41:54.730
<v Damoni Ware>He live in this house right here.
<v Damoni Ware>他住在这栋房子里。

00:41:54.730 --> 00:42:00.390
<v Ben Calhoun>Money actually lives across the street from James. Standing next to his house, Money can point to James's front door.
<v Ben Calhoun>“钱”实际上住在詹姆斯街对面。站在他的房子旁，“钱”可以指向詹姆斯的前门。

00:42:00.390 --> 00:42:13.270
<v Damoni Ware>And this one right there, you see the house with its lights on? And then it's this-- it's this house next to it. And they live in that house right there with the big brown porch. Yeah, he live in that house. His name Little James.
<v Damoni Ware>还有那栋，你看那栋灯亮着的房子？然后是那栋——旁边的那栋。他们住在那栋有大棕色门廊的房子里。对，他住在那栋房子里。他叫小詹姆斯。

00:42:13.270 --> 00:42:32.740
<v Ben Calhoun>James Williams, the kid with the big brown porch, the kid who'd been shot during the pep rally, he used to go to Harper. But he hadn't been there this year. Back on the day of the pep rally, minutes after the shooting, the first details coming in were hazy-- just that James had been taken to the hospital, and so far he was still alive.
<v Ben Calhoun>詹姆斯·威廉姆斯，那个有大棕色门廊的孩子，那个在动员会期间中枪的孩子，他以前上过哈珀。但他今年不在那里。回到动员会那天，枪击发生几分钟后，传来的第一个细节很模糊——只知道詹姆斯被送往医院，到目前为止他还活着。

00:42:32.740 --> 00:42:53.660
<v Ben Calhoun>There was already speculation about who was involved and about possible retaliation by Sixth Ward, the gang that controls the block where James and Money live. Money remembers that, as he left the pep rally, and he went to the last practice on Thursday, before the big game on Friday, he wasn't thinking about the game anymore or homecoming. He was thinking about his friend.
<v Ben Calhoun>关于谁参与其中以及控制詹姆斯和“钱”居住街区的帮派“第六区”（Sixth Ward）可能进行的报复，已经有了猜测。“钱”记得，当他离开动员会，去参加周四的最后一次练习，在周五的大赛之前，他不再考虑比赛或返校节了。他在想他的朋友。

00:42:53.660 --> 00:43:04.050
<v Damoni Ware>I didn't know-- I didn't know if he was OK or not. So that had me, like, kind of messing up in practice. Yeah. That threw off my whole day of practice.
<v Damoni Ware>我不知道——我不知道他是否没事。所以那让我，就像，练习时有点搞砸了。是的。那搞砸了我一整天的练习。

00:43:04.050 --> 00:43:14.590
<v Damoni Ware>Like, I was-- I wasn't really running. I was, like, tip toeing. Wasn't really trying to make a tackle, trying to make a play. So I was trying to worry about was he OK.
<v Damoni Ware>我没法真正跑动。我就像是踮着脚走路。没真正想去阻截，没真正想去表现。所以我当时在担心他是否安好。

00:43:14.590 --> 00:43:26.710
<v Leonetta Sanders>We don't know yet. He was up at the gas station on 67th and Damen, and we just got word that, you know, a former student was shot. So right now, he's in critical condition.
<v Leonetta Sanders>我们还不知道。他在第67街和达门大道（Damen）的加油站，我们刚接到消息，你知道，一名以前的学生被枪击了。所以现在，他在重症监护室。

00:43:26.710 --> 00:43:38.980
<v Ben Calhoun>After school on Thursday, while the football team's practicing, word's spreading about the shooting. Principal Sanders is trying to gather information. And when I find her, she's in the parking lot trying to figure out the school's response.
<v Ben Calhoun>周四放学后，橄榄球队在练习时，关于枪击的消息传播开来。桑德斯校长正试图收集信息。当我找到她时，她在停车场试图搞清楚学校的应对措施。

00:43:38.980 --> 00:43:45.040
<v Leonetta Sanders>And another thing that didn't look good-- three different gangs that came out the door, and they just kind of took off running.
<v Leonetta Sanders>另一件看起来不太好的事——三个不同的帮派从门口出来，他们就那样跑了。

00:43:45.040 --> 00:43:46.870
<v Ben Calhoun>You mean when they just came out of school here?
<v Ben Calhoun>你是说他们刚从这儿放学的时候？

00:43:46.870 --> 00:44:00.460
<v Leonetta Sanders>So they came out, went to the parking lot, and they just took off-- three different groups. For what, we don't know. So that's another indication that something's going on.
<v Leonetta Sanders>所以他们出来，去了停车场，然后他们就跑了——三个不同的团队。为了什么，我们不知道。所以那是另一件表明发生了什么事的迹象。

00:44:00.460 --> 00:44:15.880
<v Ben Calhoun>Sanders' concern is fallout from all this. The homecoming game and the dance are scheduled for the next day. They'll big school events, events where kids from other schools will be, events where security is a challenge. Sanders was worried that James Williams was just the beginning.
<v Ben Calhoun>桑德斯关心的是这一切带来的后果。返校节比赛和舞会安排在第二天。它们是大型学校活动，外校孩子也会参加，安全是一个挑战。桑德斯担心詹姆斯·威廉姆斯只是个开始。

00:44:15.880 --> 00:44:32.260
<v Leonetta Sanders>Definitely-- we have a dance tomorrow. So that's going to have a major effect, especially when they find out exactly who the shooter was. Yeah, that might have a grave impact on whether or not we even have a dance.
<v Leonetta Sanders>当然——我们明天有一个舞会。所以这会产生重大影响，特别是当他们找出到底是谁开的枪时。是的，那可能会对我们是否举办舞会产生严重影响。

00:44:32.260 --> 00:45:01.100
<v Ben Calhoun>Harper staff doesn't just struggle to protect their students from the violence in the neighborhood. They also try to protect what they see as a student's just normal high school experience, things like Homecoming and clubs and prom. Sanders said it was for these reasons that the idea of canceling the dance-- it really bothered her. But she also knew she might have to.
<v Ben Calhoun>哈珀教职工不仅努力保护他们的学生免受街区暴力侵害。他们还试图保护他们所看到的学生“正常的”高中经历，比如返校节、俱乐部和舞会。桑德斯说正是出于这些原因，取消舞会的想法——这真的让她很烦恼。但她也知道她可能不得不这么做。

00:45:01.100 --> 00:45:17.500
<v Leonetta Sanders>Good morning, Harper staff members. At this time, I need all the members for the AAR to report to the Mellon room. Once again, all members for the AAR, please report to the Mellon room at this time.
<v Leonetta Sanders>早上好，哈珀教职工成员。现在，我需要所有AAR成员到梅隆室（Mellon room）报到。再说一遍，所有AAR成员，请现在到梅隆室报到。

00:45:17.500 --> 00:45:20.560
<v Ben Calhoun>And do students know what that is when they hear it?
<v Ben Calhoun>当学生听到这个时，知道那是什么意思吗？

00:45:20.560 --> 00:45:24.820
<v Leonetta Sanders>Not really. They don't really know.
<v Leonetta Sanders>不完全知道。他们不知道。

00:45:24.820 --> 00:45:54.670
<v Ben Calhoun>Friday morning, the day after the shooting, the day of the homecoming game and dance-- this meeting that Sanders is paging people to is an AAR, short for After Action Review, the school's main meeting to figure out how to handle James Williams' shooting. The name AAR actually comes from the military. A couple of years ago, Chicago Public school officials were visiting at Fort Leavenworth in Kansas to research military training tools, tools they might use to prepare people working in the city's roughest schools.
<v Ben Calhoun>周五早上，枪击案发生后的第二天，返校节比赛和舞会的日子——桑德斯呼叫大家参加的这个会议是AAR，After Action Review（事后回顾）的缩写，学校处理詹姆斯·威廉姆斯枪击案的主要会议。AAR这个名字实际上来自军队。几年前，芝加哥公立学校的官员们访问了堪萨斯州莱文沃斯堡（Fort Leavenworth）研究军事训练工具，他们可能会用来准备在城市最艰难学校工作的人员的工具。

00:45:54.670 --> 00:46:18.370
<v Ben Calhoun>They came across the AAR, the Army's tool for analyzing events, for assessing damage, compiling information, and trying to figure out how to respond. At most schools, this situation-- a former student shot, the threat of more possible shootings-- even schools that have plans for these sorts of crises rarely have to use them. And if they end up in a situation like this, they scramble through it.
<v Ben Calhoun>他们遇到了AAR，这是军队用来分析事件、评估损失、汇编信息并试图搞清楚如何应对的工具。在大多数学校，这种情况——一名以前的学生中枪，更多潜在枪击的威胁——即使是为这类危机制定了计划的学校也很少使用它们。如果他们陷入这样的境地，他们通常是一团乱麻。

00:46:18.370 --> 00:46:40.150
<v Ben Calhoun>A district administrator told me, think about it. The natural response for a school after a shooting is to go into panic and to grieve, and then to hope that it never happens again. That's normal. And so when the Chicago schools first brought the AAR in from Fort Leavenworth and suggested it to Harper, some people said it felt wrong, like they'd be planning for students to be shot.
<v Ben Calhoun>一位学区行政人员告诉我，想想看。学校在枪击案后的自然反应是陷入恐慌和悲伤，然后希望再也不要发生。那是正常的。所以当芝加哥学校第一次从莱文沃斯堡引入AAR并向哈珀建议时，有些人说感觉不对劲，好像他们是在策划学生被枪杀一样。

00:46:40.150 --> 00:46:47.410
<v Ben Calhoun>But the staff at Harper knew it would be better to have procedures and a plan to contain the damage. That's the AAR.
<v Ben Calhoun>但哈珀的员工知道有一个流程和计划来控制损害会更好。那就是AAR。

00:46:47.410 --> 00:46:50.810
<v Leonetta Sanders>Marcel, Coach, any word on how James is doing?
<v Leonetta Sanders>马塞尔（Marcel），教练，有詹姆斯状况如何的消息吗？

00:46:50.810 --> 00:46:52.150
<v Man>No.
<v Man>没有。

00:46:52.150 --> 00:46:54.780
<v Leonetta Sanders>OK. Did he have surgery yesterday, or you don't know?
<v Leonetta Sanders>好的。他昨天手术了吗，还是你不知道？

00:46:54.780 --> 00:46:57.430
<v Man>I don't know.
<v Man>我不知道。

00:46:57.430 --> 00:47:11.240
<v Ben Calhoun>Harper's AAR meeting is held first thing the day after the shooting. One of the first parts of it is just whiteboarding the social and family relationships of the victim to see who could and would be affected and to consider how.
<v Ben Calhoun>哈珀的AAR会议在枪击案后的第一天举行。其中的一部分仅仅是在白板上写下受害者的社交和家庭关系，看看谁可能会受到影响，以及如何考虑。

00:47:11.240 --> 00:47:30.040
<v Leonetta Sanders>OK. So we all know James was shot yesterday right when were getting out, member of Sixth Ward. So I had heard stories about his brother, Jaman, in the neighborhood. Anybody got any word on how he's doing, or--
<v Leonetta Sanders>好的。所以我们都知道詹姆斯昨天放学时中枪了，是第六区的成员。所以我听过关于他弟弟贾曼（Jaman）在街区的传闻。有人收到他如何的消息吗，或者——

00:47:30.040 --> 00:47:47.200
<v Ben Calhoun>The group includes all the deans of the school-- social workers, the football coach, guidance counselors, the psychologist, about two dozen people all together. And the conversation moves pretty fast. They summarize the information they've been able to gather. They list kids with connections to the three gangs involved.
<v Ben Calhoun>该小组包括学校所有的院长——社会工作者、橄榄球教练、辅导员、心理学家，总共大约两打人。谈话进行得很快。他们总结了他们能够收集到的信息。他们列出了与三个涉及帮派有联系的孩子。

00:47:47.200 --> 00:47:52.490
<v Leonetta Sanders>So we already know, number one, that we need to pull all those guys. Can we name them?
<v Leonetta Sanders>所以我们已经知道，第一，我们需要把那些人全部找出来。我们可以叫出他们的名字吗？

00:47:52.490 --> 00:47:52.980
<v Leonetta Sanders>[SCHOOL BELL RINGING]
<v Leonetta Sanders>[学校铃声]

00:47:52.980 --> 00:47:54.940
<v Leonetta Sanders>Who are they?
<v Leonetta Sanders>他们是谁？

00:47:54.940 --> 00:47:58.810
<v Ben Calhoun>People rattle off names around the room, kids that'll be pulled out of class later.
<v Ben Calhoun>人们在房间里报出名字，这些孩子稍后会被从课上叫走。

00:47:58.810 --> 00:47:59.930
<v Leonetta Sanders>Any freshmen?
<v Leonetta Sanders>有新生吗？

00:47:59.930 --> 00:48:26.160
<v Ben Calhoun>The school just wants to ask them what's going on, what might happen, and to get them out of the building, especially at dismissal time, when things could get dangerous. Money, the football player, James's friend from across the street, they want to ask him what's been happening on the block. They talk about following what kids are saying online and who will do that. A faculty member named Marcel Smith says the students have realized that the school has been monitoring Facebook. They've stopped posting stuff there.
<v Ben Calhoun>学校只是想问问他们发生了什么，可能发生什么，并让他们离开学校，尤其是在放学时间，那时可能会很危险。“钱”，那个橄榄球运动员，詹姆斯的街对面朋友，他们想问他街区里一直发生什么。他们谈论关注孩子在网上说的话，以及谁会这样做。一位叫马塞尔·史密斯的教职工说，学生们已经意识到学校一直在监控Facebook。他们已经不再在那里发东西了。

00:48:26.160 --> 00:48:31.350
<v Marcel Smith>And real quick, Principal Sanders, so since they know we on Facebook, everybody's tweeting.
<v Marcel Smith>很快，桑德斯校长，因为他们知道我们在Facebook上，每个人都在发推文。

00:48:31.350 --> 00:48:37.510
<v Leonetta Sanders>So we ain't got no tweet account with them? Who got a tweet account? So they've done [INAUDIBLE] our game, now?
<v Leonetta Sanders>所以我们没有他们的推特账号？谁有推特账号？所以他们已经搞定[听不清]我们的游戏了，现在？

00:48:37.510 --> 00:48:38.480
<v Leonetta Sanders>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Leonetta Sanders>[不同声音交叉]

00:48:38.480 --> 00:48:38.970
<v Woman>Yeah, they off it.
<v Woman>是的，他们不用那个了。

00:48:38.970 --> 00:48:41.920
<v Leonetta Sanders>They off of Facebook now?
<v Leonetta Sanders>他们现在不用Facebook了吗？

00:48:41.920 --> 00:48:58.780
<v Ben Calhoun>There's also the issue of James's brother. James left Harper the year before, but his younger brother Jamon still attends. He's not in school that day, but they talk about having someone check on him, and also having someone explain to the kids in each of his classes what happened. They talk about the kids who took off running the night before.
<v Ben Calhoun>还有詹姆斯弟弟的问题。詹姆斯去年离开了哈珀，但他的弟弟贾蒙（Jamon）还在上学。他今天没来上学，但他们谈论让某人去查看他，并让某人向他每个班的孩子解释发生了什么。他们谈论前一天晚上跑掉的孩子。

00:48:58.780 --> 00:49:00.250
<v Leonetta Sanders>--running down the street.
<v Leonetta Sanders>——跑在街上。

00:49:00.250 --> 00:49:05.170
<v Ben Calhoun>Also on the table is the issue of homecoming. Sanders is visibly unsure.
<v Ben Calhoun>同样在讨论桌上的是返校节的问题。桑德斯明显很拿不准。

00:49:05.170 --> 00:49:14.350
<v Leonetta Sanders>If I have not said this, I probably need to say this. So I need as many people at this dance tonight as possible.
<v Leonetta Sanders>如果我没说过这个，我可能需要说一下。我需要今晚舞会上的人尽可能多。

00:49:14.350 --> 00:49:28.700
<v Ben Calhoun>Sanders is married to a Chicago police officer. And so are some other people on staff. Since the previous day, she's been trying to round up off-duty police officers to volunteer as security at the dance. She's also asked Central Office for additional security.
<v Ben Calhoun>桑德斯的丈夫是一名芝加哥警察。这也是学校里一些其他人的丈夫的职业。自前一天以来，她一直在试图召集休班警察作为舞会的志愿保安。她也向中心办公室申请了额外安保。

00:49:28.700 --> 00:49:53.020
<v Leonetta Sanders>But yeah, I need as many people tonight as possible. And when we have dances and things like this, no, I can't pay you. But your presence is definitely needed. Because we need people to watch inside so that nothing happens. As well as-- and I usually have the security, the men, standing outside along with police.
<v Leonetta Sanders>但真的，我今晚需要尽可能多的人。当我们有舞会之类的活动时，不，我不能付钱给你。但你的出席是绝对需要的。因为我们需要人盯着内部，这样什么都不会发生。还有——我通常有保安，这些男士，站在外面和警察一起。

00:49:53.020 --> 00:49:58.930
<v Ben Calhoun>As everyone leaves, Sanders tell someone on staff she's worried about security at the homecoming game itself.
<v Ben Calhoun>当每个人离开时，桑德斯告诉员工她担心返校节比赛本身的安全。

00:49:58.930 --> 00:50:04.450
<v Leonetta Sanders>Because my issue is, if they believe the Breeds did that, half of my football team are Breeds.
<v Leonetta Sanders>因为我的问题是，如果他们认为Breeds帮做了那件事，我一半的橄榄球队员都是Breeds帮的。

00:50:04.450 --> 00:50:08.120
<v Woman>Mhm. [INAUDIBLE].
<v Woman>嗯。[听不清]。

00:50:08.120 --> 00:50:27.150
<v Leonetta Sanders>So you know what I'm saying? It's not like they pat them down at the games. I don't never see them patting nobody down at the game. You know what I'm saying, I'm, like, so that's hard to-- I don't know. I don't know. I don't know. I don't know.
<v Leonetta Sanders>所以你知道我在说什么吗？比赛的时候又不搜身。我从没见过他们在比赛中搜身。你知道我在说什么，我，就像，所以这很难——我不知道。我不知道。我不知道。我不知道。

00:50:27.150 --> 00:50:52.280
<v Ben Calhoun>Just so it's clear, what Principal Sanders is saying is that half of her football players have ties to the gangs who are supposedly behind the shooting. And obviously, that's worrisome. Because they might become targets, and they're at the center of every large school event she has planned in the next 24 hours.
<v Ben Calhoun>为了清楚起见，桑德斯校长说的是，她的一半橄榄球运动员与据称是枪击事件背后的帮派有联系。显然，这很令人担忧。因为他们可能成为目标，而且他们是她计划在未来24小时内举办的每场大型学校活动的核心。

00:50:52.280 --> 00:50:58.760
<v Antoine Reed>OK. You want to talk to 'em or no? Get over here and talk to Mama.
<v Antoine Reed>好的。你想和他们谈谈还是不？过来跟妈妈谈谈。

00:50:58.760 --> 00:51:20.890
<v Ben Calhoun>All day long, Harper's staff implements the plan from the AAR meeting. For Coach Antoine Reed and others, that means spending the day gathering information, pulling students out of class. It's an unusual situation-- sending kids home not because they've done anything wrong, but as a matter of prevention. This student here still hadn't quite figured out that he wasn't in trouble.
<v Ben Calhoun>整整一天，哈珀的教职工都在执行AAR会议的计划。对于教练安托万·里德（Antoine Reed）和其他人来说，这意味着花一天时间收集信息，把学生从课上叫走。这是一个不寻常的情况——让孩子回家不是因为他们做了错事，而是作为一种预防措施。这个学生甚至还没完全搞清楚他没惹麻烦。

00:51:20.890 --> 00:51:31.380
<v Antoine Reed>Until I say-- did I ever say you was in trouble? If you are in trouble, who's the first person you think you're going to see? I don't care where you at. I don't care if you on this roof and you get in trouble. The first person you're going to see is me.
<v Antoine Reed>直到我——我有没有说过你惹麻烦了？如果你惹麻烦了，你觉得你会见到的第一个人是谁？我不在乎你在哪儿。我不在乎你是否在屋顶上惹麻烦了。你要见到的第一个人是我。

00:51:31.380 --> 00:51:40.150
<v Antoine Reed>You wasn't in trouble. We understand what's going on and that issue is not resolved yet. He's still in the hospital. Everybody is still angry.
<v Antoine Reed>你没惹麻烦。我们了解正在发生什么，那个问题还没解决。他还在医院。大家都很生气。

00:51:40.150 --> 00:51:52.730
<v Antoine Reed>And we are not taking any chances, you know what I'm saying? So it's not that you in trouble. It's that we're taking precautionary measures. We know how it's going to look today at the end of the day. Let's not be naive. Me and you live around here.
<v Antoine Reed>我们不冒险，你明白我在说什么吗？所以不是你惹麻烦了。而是我们正在采取预防措施。我们知道今天结束时会是什么样子。别天真了。我和你就住这附近。

00:51:52.730 --> 00:52:03.050
<v Male Student>Yeah.
<v Male Student>对。

00:52:03.050 --> 00:52:14.090
<v Ben Calhoun>Throughout the day, Principal Sanders shores up the security plan for the game and also for the dance. She's disappointed that more Harper staff don't agree to stay late to act as security.
<v Ben Calhoun>整整一天，桑德斯校长加强了比赛和舞会的安保计划。她很失望没有更多的哈珀员工同意留下来加班做安保。

00:52:14.090 --> 00:52:16.430
<v Ben Calhoun>You're not going outside at the end of school today?
<v Ben Calhoun>你今天放学后不出去吗？

00:52:16.430 --> 00:52:20.750
<v Anita Stewart>No, I'm going to go outside and get in my car and go home.
<v Anita Stewart>不，我要出去上车回家。

00:52:20.750 --> 00:52:37.870
<v Ben Calhoun>This is social worker Anita Stewart. Anita tells me she won't be staying late. Just like Coach Reed, she'd spent the day talking to kids about what happened, and she's scared. Anita doesn't want to let Harper down, but with a family and kids of her own, she decided she isn't up for it.
<v Ben Calhoun>这是社会工作者安妮塔·斯图尔特（Anita Stewart）。安妮塔告诉我她不会留下来。就像里德教练一样，她花了一天时间跟孩子们谈论发生的事，她很害怕。安妮塔不想让哈珀失望，但她自己也有家庭和孩子，她决定她没法留下来。

00:52:37.870 --> 00:52:56.080
<v Anita Stewart>I'm not going to, you know, go out and stand around. There's too much going on. And I think I got fair warning from the kids to stay out of the neighborhood. And the kids are telling me stay out of the neighborhood. And I'm going to ride with the kids and believe what they're telling me.
<v Anita Stewart>我不会，你知道，出去站在那儿。这儿发生的事太多了。我想我从孩子们那里得到了充分的警告，让我远离这个街区。孩子们告诉我要远离这个街区。我要听孩子们的，相信他们告诉我的。

00:52:56.080 --> 00:53:15.490
<v Ben Calhoun>This isn't typical for her. Anita spends a lot of time trying to intervene with kids so they don't end up shooting each other. She's constantly going out into the neighborhood around Harper, after school and on weekends, talking kids down from fighting, going to rival cliques and mediating. Reminding everyone, this is stupid. Stop it.
<v Ben Calhoun>这对她来说不典型。安妮塔花了很多时间试图介入孩子们的冲突，这样他们就不会互相枪击。她经常在放学后和周末去哈珀周围的社区，劝阻打架的孩子，去敌对小团体那里调解。提醒大家，这很蠢。别再干了。

00:53:15.490 --> 00:53:27.260
<v Ben Calhoun>But today, Anita is struggling. She's sitting in her office, trying to deal with the reality that no matter how much she or anyone does, no matter how much they hope, inevitably, there will be more violence.
<v Ben Calhoun>但今天，安妮塔在挣扎。她坐在办公室里，试图面对一个现实：无论她或任何人做什么，无论他们抱有多大希望，必然会有更多暴力发生。

00:53:27.260 --> 00:53:41.950
<v Anita Stewart>The hardest thing is knowing that something is getting ready to happen, and you can't stop it. That hurts. And it's, like, because you don't know where it's going to happen. And you know that the strike is getting ready to come. But you don't know where it's coming. And it hurts.
<v Anita Stewart>最难的是知道事情即将发生，但你却无法阻止它。这很痛。而且，就像，因为你不知道它会在哪儿发生。你知道攻击即将到来。但你不知道它从哪儿来。这很痛。

00:53:41.950 --> 00:53:43.060
<v Anita Stewart>[CRYING]
<v Anita Stewart>[哭泣声]

00:53:43.060 --> 00:54:12.060
<v Anita Stewart>It hurts because they not-- the kids, they're not bad. So you know that something is getting ready to happen, because-- [SNIFFLING] the kids, they tell you, and it's, like, you can't stop it, because you don't know where it's going to happen. And I'm trying to do what I can. [SOBBING]
<v Anita Stewart>这很痛，因为他们不是——孩子们，他们不坏。所以你知道事情即将发生，因为——[吸鼻涕]孩子们，他们告诉你，但这就像，你无法阻止，因为你不知道它会在哪儿发生。我正在尽我所能。[啜泣]

00:54:12.060 --> 00:54:25.730
<v Ben Calhoun>Just before dismissal, just like Anita feared, there's news of more shooting. A parent and a student were on their way home, and someone fired shots. Nobody was hit, but it looks like it might have been retaliation.
<v Ben Calhoun>放学前，就像安妮塔担心的那样，有更多枪击的消息。一名家长和学生在回家的路上，有人开了枪。没人中弹，但这看起来像是报复。

00:54:25.730 --> 00:54:55.300
<v Ben Calhoun>And so the school has to decide what to do next-- cancel the homecoming game, cancel the dance, or risk it. Push back against the violence, and try to preserve a little bit of high school normalcy for the students. Every option they have seems like a bad option. And it's getting late in the day. They have to decide all of this, and they've got to decide soon.
<v Ben Calhoun>所以学校必须决定下一步做什么——取消返校节比赛，取消舞会，还是冒险一试。反抗暴力，为学生保留一点高中生活的正常感。他们拥有的每一个选择看起来都是个糟糕的选择。天色已晚。他们必须决定这一切，而且他们必须尽快决定。

00:54:55.300 --> 00:56:00.010
<v Ira Glass>Ben Calhoun is one of the producers of our show. What Principal Sanders and the staff decide to do and how their plan works out, whether they contain the violence-- next week, Alex Kotlowitz, Linda Lutton, and Ben Calhoun will be back. They'll pick up our story where we're leaving it off today, with the second half of our story from Harper High School in Chicago. I hope you join us. I appreciate you in advance for that.
<v Ira Glass>本·卡尔霍恩（Ben Calhoun）是我们节目的制作人之一。桑德斯校长和教职工决定做什么，他们的计划如何实施，他们是否遏制了暴力——下周，亚历克斯·科特洛维茨、琳达·勒顿和本·卡尔霍恩将回来。他们将从今天结束的地方继续讲述我们在芝加哥哈珀高中的故事。我希望你加入我们。我预先感谢你的收听。

00:56:00.010 --> 00:56:26.840
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by our senior producer Julie Snyder, with Alex Blumberg, Ben Calhoun, Sarah Koenig, Miki Meek, Jonathan Menjivar, Lisa Pollak, Brian Reed, Robyn Semien, Alissa Shipp, and Nancy Updike. Production help from Phia Bennin. Seth Lind is our operations director. Emily Condon's our production manager. Elise Bergerson's our administrative assistant. Original scoring for today's program by the Late Bloomer. Music help from Damian Graf and Rob Geddis. Thanks today to [? Coby ?] Williams, Jens Ludwig, Roseanna Ander, [? Joe ?] Jennings, Michelle Harris, [? Rob ?] [INAUDIBLE], Julie Beer.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目由我们的高级制作人朱莉·斯奈德（Julie Snyder）制作，亚历克斯·布隆伯格（Alex Blumberg）、本·卡尔霍恩、萨拉·柯尼格（Sarah Koenig）、米基·米克（Miki Meek）、乔纳森·门吉瓦尔（Jonathan Menjivar）、丽莎·波拉克（Lisa Pollak）、布莱恩·里德（Brian Reed）、罗宾·塞米恩（Robyn Semien）、阿丽莎·希普（Alissa Shipp）和南希·厄普代克（Nancy Updike）协助。菲亚·本宁（Phia Bennin）提供制作帮助。塞思·林德（Seth Lind）是我们的运营总监。艾米丽·康登（Emily Condon）是我们的制作经理。埃莉斯·伯格森（Elise Bergerson）是我们的行政助理。今天节目的原创配乐由Late Bloomer创作。音乐协助来自达米安·格拉夫（Damian Graf）和罗布·格迪斯（Rob Geddis）。感谢今天的科比·威廉姆斯（Coby Williams）、延斯·路德维希（Jens Ludwig）、罗珊娜·安德（Roseanna Ander）、乔·詹宁斯（Joe Jennings）、米歇尔·哈里斯（Michelle Harris）、罗布·[听不清]、朱莉·比尔（Julie Beer）。

00:56:26.840 --> 00:56:39.960
<v Ira Glass>And special thanks to, of course, Harper principal Leonetta Sanders, head football coach Maris Carroll, social workers Crystal Smith and Anita Stewart, and the rest of the staff, students, and families at Harper High School for being so generous with their time.
<v Ira Glass>当然，特别感谢哈珀校长莱奥内塔·桑德斯，主教练马里斯·卡罗尔（Maris Carroll），社会工作者克里斯托·史密斯和安妮塔·斯图尔特，以及哈珀高中的教职工、学生和家庭，感谢他们如此慷慨地贡献时间。

00:56:39.960 --> 00:56:54.470
<v Ira Glass>Our website, ThisAmericanLife.org. This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International. WBEZ management oversight for our program by our boss, Mr. Torey Malatia. I don't know, I keep forgetting to wear my belt to work. I don't know. I don't see what the big deal is. He will not let it go.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站，ThisAmericanLife.org。《美国生活》由公共广播国际发行。WBEZ对我们节目的管理监督由我们的老板，托里·马拉蒂亚（Torey Malatia）先生负责。我不知道，我总是忘了系皮带去上班。我不知道。我没觉得那有什么大不了的。他就是不肯罢休。

00:56:54.470 --> 00:57:00.180
<v Leonetta Sanders>We are not selling crack. Pull your pants up. Nobody wants to see the crack of your butt, baby.
<v Leonetta Sanders>我们又不是在卖可卡因。把你的裤子提起来。没人想看到你的屁股缝，宝贝。

00:57:00.180 --> 00:57:05.180
<v Ben Calhoun>I'm Ira Glass, back next week with Part 2. Be there for more stories of This American Life.
<v Ben Calhoun>我是艾拉·格拉斯，下周回来播出第2部分。在那里收听更多《美国生活》的故事。
